virglrenderer: integer outputs are written through a float temporary

What: the guest's TGSI writes results straight to integer outputs (integer
colour buffers, gl_SampleMask, gl_FragStencilRefARB, gl_Layer,
gl_ViewportIndex, gl_PrimitiveID): it folds floatBitsToUint(f(x)) or uint(i)
into "OP OUT[n], ...". Each instruction's GLSL then had to know the output's
type, and many did not: float math converted the value (uint(1.0 / x)),
integer ops went through uintBitsToFloat() and back, texture fetches, queries,
image/buffer loads, CMP, SEQ... did not compile. Now every such output is
written like a float temporary (vec4 int_out_tmp<n>, same GLSL as for a TEMP
register, for every opcode, named after the TGSI register) and stored once
with its bits (floatBitsToUint/Int) at every return and before every vertex a
geometry shader emits. Which components a shader writes comes from tgsi_scan
(new: output_writemask), so a store before the write in the text (a loop) is
not missed, and only those are stored to a colour output (with framebuffer
fetch the others keep the fetched value). gl_FragStencilRefARB comes from .y,
the others from .x; gl_SampleMask only [0] (hosts refuse [1..3] for 32 samples
or fewer). Precise results keep precise on their temporary. Reads of the output
read the temporary (not FBFETCH). Float outputs and temporaries get the same
GLSL as before.
Not covered (they keep upstream's per-instruction GLSL): integer outputs
declared as an array range, every output of a shader that reads or writes any
output with an indirect index (the index could reach past a temporary), and
integer colour outputs with logic ops (they have their own temporaries); no
guest driver we know sends these to integer outputs.
Why: dEQP-GLES3 builtin_functions.precision.sqrt/length read 315 for
sqrt(99463) instead of its bits; the shaders that did not compile stopped the
guest's whole context. Same on Linux hosts.
Upstream bug (same code upstream); upstreamable; not sent.
Applies after virgl-shader-core-glsl-version.patch.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-core-glsl-shaders.c: 18 opcode kinds (float math,
compares, conversions, integer ops, TEX, TXF, TXQ, and image/buffer LOAD, RESQ,
atomics as text only: the Mac has no images) into uint and int colour outputs,
an integer op into gl_SampleMask, gl_Layer from a geometry shader (also written
after an EmitVertex in a loop), colour outputs declared in reverse order, a
full-writemask gl_SampleMask, the stencil value, an output also written or
read indirectly, partial writes (also after a framebuffer fetch), a precise
write; compiled by the Mac's OpenGL where it has the feature; all fail without
this patch.
